    "content": "You are comparing two texts written in response to the same task. Decide which one reads MORE like unedited, default output from an AI language model.\n\nJudge ONLY the presence of the tells listed below. Do not judge argument quality, factual accuracy, length, formatting preference, or whether you agree with the content. A longer, better-argued or more polished text is not more human. Pick the text with MORE tells.\n\nTELLS OF UNEDITED AI PROSE (judge on these only)\n\nStructure\n1. Negative parallelism used as a device: \"It's not X. It's Y.\" / \"not just X, but Y\" / \"isn't about X, it's about Y\", especially more than once.\n2. Signposting: the text announces what it is about to do (\"Let's break this down\", \"Here's the thing\", \"Here's why\", \"The bottom line\", \"The takeaway\").\n3. Self-applause: \"Let that sink in\", \"This changes everything\", \"Read that again\", \"Make no mistake\".\n4. Recap endings: a closing paragraph that restates the body (\"In conclusion\", \"Ultimately\", \"At the end of the day\", \"In short\").\n5. Bold-label paragraph openers (\"**The problem.** ...\") and headers, bullets or numbered lists where prose was asked for.\n6. Triads everywhere: lists of exactly three items or three adjectives, sentence after sentence.\n7. Participial tails: sentences ending in \", making it ...\", \", ensuring that ...\", \", allowing teams to ...\".\n8. Invented labels for ordinary observations (\"I call this the Clarity Flywheel\").\n9. Uniform rhythm: every sentence about the same length, or relentless short punchy fragments. Real writing has one sentence that runs on and one short one.\n10. Rhetorical question followed by its own answer (\"The result? Nobody used it.\").\n\nWords\n11. Overused vocabulary: delve, tapestry, myriad, underscore, seamless, robust, foster, elevate, unlock, testament, landscape, navigate, crucial, pivotal, leverage, harness, realm, embark, journey, transformative, paradigm, ever-evolving, multifaceted, holistic, resonate, empower, unleash, beacon, intricate, nuanced, vibrant, meticulous, streamline, game-changer, cutting-edge, actionable, supercharge, north star, move the needle, \"in today's fast-paced world\", \"it's worth noting\", \"plays a crucial role\".\n12. Filler transitions at sentence start: However, Additionally, Furthermore, Moreover, That said, Ultimately, Importantly, Indeed, Of course.\n13. Em dashes used as the default joint between clauses.\n14. Artificial enthusiasm: \"thrilled to announce\", \"excited to share\", \"we've got you covered\", \"say goodbye to\", \"buckle up\".\n\nReader\n15. Performing for an imagined crowd rather than talking to one person; nothing specific (no number, name, date, or concrete detail) for several sentences in a row.\n16. Generic or manufactured anecdotes: a story with no specific details that could only be true of one person.\n17. Every point lands, every ending uplifts; the author is never irritated, uncertain, or wrong about anything.\n18. Explaining what the reader could infer from the previous sentence (\"what this means is ...\").\n\n\nTASK GIVEN TO BOTH WRITERS:\nWrite an essay of up to 500 words on why most product roadmaps fail.\n\nPlain prose only: no headers, no bullet points, no bold text, no em dashes. Write like a person.\n\n=== TEXT A ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they are asked to do too many jobs at once. A single document is supposed to reassure the board, motivate engineers, keep sales from overpromising, and give customers a reason to renew. Those audiences want different things, and a roadmap that tries to please all of them ends up as a list of features with dates attached, which is the one form guaranteed to disappoint everyone.\n\nThe dates are the first problem. Software estimation is famously bad, but roadmaps treat it as if it were reliable enough to publish a year out. Someone picks a quarter for a feature because the slide needs a quarter, and that guess hardens into a commitment the moment a salesperson repeats it to a prospect. When the date slips, and it almost always slips, the team looks incompetent even if they made the right call to spend those weeks on something more urgent. The roadmap becomes a record of broken promises rather than a description of intent.\n\nThe second problem is that roadmaps are usually built from the inside out. They start with what the company wants to ship, not with what the customer is struggling to do. A feature gets on the list because an executive likes it, or a big account asked for it, or a competitor announced it last month. None of those are terrible reasons on their own, but stacked together they produce a plan with no center of gravity. Nobody can say what the product is trying to become, only what it will contain. And a plan with no thesis cannot tell you what to cut when things get tight, so nothing gets cut and everything ships late and half finished.\n\nThird, roadmaps assume the world will hold still. They are written with the information available in January and then defended through December as if nothing was learned in between. In reality a good team learns something important every few weeks, from support tickets, usage data, a lost deal, a competitor stumbling. The healthy response is to change course. But changing the roadmap feels like admitting the original was wrong, so teams either quietly ignore it or spend energy negotiating amendments instead of building. Either way the document stops reflecting what people actually do.\n\nThere is also a quieter failure, which is that the roadmap gets mistaken for the strategy. Writing down a sequence of deliverables feels like thinking, but it is not the same as deciding who you are for, what you will refuse to build, and what you believe about where the market is heading. Without that layer, the roadmap is just a to do list wearing a suit.\n\nThe roadmaps that work tend to be humbler. They commit firmly to the near term, sketch the middle distance in terms of problems rather than features, and leave the far horizon deliberately vague. They say why before they say what. And they are treated as a snapshot of current thinking, revised without shame, rather than a contract to be enforced. That is less satisfying to present in a meeting, but it has the advantage of being true.\n=== END OF TEXT A ===\n\n=== TEXT B ===\nMost product roadmaps fail because they pretend to offer certainty in a situation defined by uncertainty. They turn assumptions about customers, markets, technology, and organizational capacity into a sequence of promised features and dates. Once those promises are written down and shared, the roadmap stops being a tool for learning and becomes a contract that teams are expected to honor.\n\nThis creates several problems. First, roadmaps often focus on outputs rather than outcomes. They describe what the team will build, not what should change for the customer or the business. Shipping a new dashboard, integration, or workflow may look like progress, but it means little if adoption, retention, revenue, or customer satisfaction remain unchanged. The team can complete the roadmap and still fail to improve the product.\n\nSecond, many roadmaps are assembled through negotiation rather than strategy. Sales wants features for prospective customers. Executives want visible initiatives. Support wants fixes for common complaints. Engineering wants to address technical debt. Product managers try to satisfy everyone, producing a crowded list with no clear set of choices. A roadmap that includes every request does not communicate priorities. It reveals the absence of them.\n\nThird, roadmaps usually underestimate the cost of discovery and change. Teams learn after work begins. Customer needs prove more complicated than expected, technical constraints appear, competitors move, and business priorities shift. Yet organizations often treat any adjustment as poor execution. Teams then keep building low-value features because changing direction would require admitting that the original plan was wrong.\n\nRoadmaps also fail when they are disconnected from capacity. Leaders may plan as if every team member is available for new development, ignoring maintenance, incidents, compliance work, defects, and coordination. Dates become optimistic guesses, dependencies multiply, and delays cascade. Confidence falls, leading to more status meetings and tighter control rather than better decisions.\n\nA useful roadmap should not claim to predict the future. It should explain the problems the organization believes are worth solving, the outcomes it hopes to create, and the evidence that would justify continued investment. Near-term work can be specific, while longer-term plans should remain flexible. Assumptions should be visible, priorities should involve real tradeoffs, and teams should be allowed to change course when they learn something important.\n\nMost product roadmaps fail not because planning is useless, but because they confuse planning with prediction. The purpose of a roadmap is to align people around direction while preserving the ability to learn. When it becomes a fixed feature schedule, it offers the comfort of certainty at the cost of building the right product.\n=== END OF TEXT B ===\n\nAnswer with a single JSON object and nothing else:\n{\"more_ai\": \"A\" or \"B\", \"confidence\": 1-5, \"tells_A\": [\"short tell\", ...], \"tells_B\": [\"short tell\", ...]}\n"
